3, Comparison Analysis
Cite 1: 
The summary of Cite 1
Cite 1 provides a method to correct the artifact on a face generated by diffusion model. It is a solution to correct the artifact generated by AI model. The input of this method is a face with artifacts generated by diffusion model. By identifying an attribute of the artifacts position, selecting a value for the attribute associated with artifact position (like mouth and eyes) to be closed. Using the selecting value and a generation network to close the related position and give an intermediate image (For example, if the artifact is on the mouth, this operation will generate a face with a closed mouth.). Meanwhile, another value is selected associated with the artifact position to be opened on the intermediate image. This value and the intermediate image are passed to the image generation model to open the related position and give the corrected image without artifact. (For example, if the artifact is on the mouth, this operation will open the mouth based on the intermediate image and give the corrected image). Further, generating a high-resolution image based on the corrected image using a super-resolution network. Finally, identifying the area in the image including additional artifacts and give the corresponding mask image. According to the mask image, an inpainting network is used to generate the final corrected image without artifacts.
 
Fig. 6 The working flow of Cite 1

Difference:
The differences with Cite 1 are listed as follows:
1）The purposes are different.  
Cite 1 is designed to correct artifacts in AI-generated portraits (not real portraits), such as asymmetrical eye sizes or a split in the mouth. Since these are not real faces, there is no requirement for fidelity; the goal is simply to repair the image so that it is free of artifacts. Cite 1 is a method for correcting artifacts in AI-generated images.
In contrast, our invention aims to improve the quality of a low-quality (noisy, blurry, and with lost details) and real (with a genuine ID) facial image, enhancing its image quality to make it clean, noise-free, and with clear textures. Since it deals with real portraits, there is a strong requirement for fidelity (the image must resemble the actual person), and the method also prevents the introduction of artifacts during the quality enhancement process. Our invention is a method for enhancing the quality of user-captured photos while preserving fidelity.

2）Technical solutions are different. 
Diffusion models are a type of general-purpose generative neural network widely applied in various visual generation tasks. However, when used for image restoration, they present two challenges: 1) the restored images may lack fidelity; 2) artifacts may be generated.

Cite 1 employs this technology to correct artifacts in pre-AI-generated images (which are not real images) without addressing the issue of image fidelity. The process includes two steps: 1) generating an intermediate image, which essentially involves locating these artifact regions; 2) using the diffusion model to correct the intermediate image, essentially regenerating the content in the located artifact regions. However, this approach does not fundamentally resolve the problem of artifacts potentially introduced by diffusion techniques, as new artifacts may be generated.

Our invention also utilizes diffusion model technology to enhance the quality of a low-quality image while ensuring authenticity. The specific differences between our invention and Cite 1 include: 1) our invention uses a high-quality reference facial image to extract personalized features that match the individual's identity, modulating the diffusion process to ensure fidelity at the macro ID level. It also extracts texture features from the reference face and performs localized texture corrections on the diffusion results to ensure fidelity at the micro texture level. 2) It predicts the potential artifacts in the low-quality face and generates negative samples that are fed into the diffusion model to guide the diffusion process in avoiding artifact generation.

The innovation of our invention does not lie in the use of diffusion model technology itself, but rather in how it leverages a reference image to extract critical information to improve restoration fidelity and how it generates negative samples to guide the diffusion process in avoiding artifact generation. These distinguishing features are all not disclosed in Cite 1.

3）The definition of the intermediate face is different.
In Cite 1, the intermediate image refers to an image generated by a machine learning model based on the original image. This intermediate face shows the areas of the face with artifacts in a closed state (for example, if there are artifacts around the eyes, the generated intermediate face will have closed eyes; if there are artifacts around the mouth, the mouth will appear closed in the intermediate face). The model then generates a corrected face image based on this intermediate face. The corrected face image displays those areas of the face in an open state and free of artifacts. In short, the intermediate face in Cite 1 is an image where the artifact-affected areas are shown in a closed state, and its specific appearance depends on the location of the artifacts in the input face.

In contrast, the intermediate face in our invention is a representation of an "average face" image feature that lacks personalized characteristics. It is artifact-free and is pre-learned and stored in a dictionary of image features, independent of the artifacts present in the input face.

4）The role of the intermediate face is different. 
In Cite 1, the intermediate face serves as the input for the restoration model. The restoration model corrects the closed areas of the intermediate face to generate a final face image where these areas are in an open position and free of artifacts.

In our invention, the neutral face (not intermediate face) is used to extract personalized features from a high-quality reference face (personalized features = reference face features - average face features). This extraction provides fidelity information for the face restoration process.

Cite 2: 
The summary of Cite 2
The Cite 2 converts a generic 3D face model to a personalized 3D face model based on the extracted feature from two or multi 2D input image at different views by comparing a texture pattern of the input face image to a reference personalized 3D model to refine the face model until a similarity threshold is met. Specially, feature points of a face from input images comprising a first face image and a second face image are extracted and then the personalized 3D face model projects to each of the first face image and the second face image. Last, refining the personalized 3D face model is based on a difference in texture patterns between the first face image to which the personalized 3D face model is projected and the second face image to which the personalized 3D face model is projected. The working flow is shown in Fig.7.
 
