Difference:
The differences with Cite 2 are listed as follows:
1）The purposes are different. 
 
Fig. 8 Different purposes
Cite 2's purpose is to create a 3D facial model by using identical feature points in two or more 2D facial images at various angles.
Our invention aims to improve the image quality of a low-quality 2D facial image by utilizing fidelity information derived from a high-quality reference 2D facial image, identifying its specific features via the correspondence relationships defined within the 3D geometric model.

2）Technical solutions are different. 
Cite 2, which is a non-AI method, outlines a process for creating a personalized 3D face model from two input images of the same face taken from different angles. Here's a breakdown of the process: Feature Point Extraction -> Deforming a Generic 3D Model -> Projecting the 3D Model -> Refining the 3D Model. The refinement process involves comparing the texture patterns (surface details like skin texture) around corresponding points on the face in both images. If there’s a difference in these texture patterns, the 3D model is further refined.

Our invention utilizes AI model to enhance the quality of a low-quality image while ensuring authenticity. The specific innovation points include: 1) Our invention uses a high-quality reference facial image to extract personalized features that match the individual's identity, modulating the diffusion process to ensure fidelity at the macro ID level. It also extracts texture features from the reference face and performs localized texture corrections on the diffusion results to ensure fidelity at the micro texture level. 2) It predicts the potential artifacts in the low-quality face and generates negative samples that are fed into the diffusion model to guide the diffusion process in avoiding artifact generation. These distinguishing features are all not disclosed in Cite 1.  In addition, although both the two solutions mention items related to 3D face model, deformation and texture, they have different function and meaning. Among them, the most critical concept is 3D face model. Constructing 3D face model is goal of Cite 2, whereas in our invention, the 3D cues serve as an intermediate feature containing corresponding relation between different images. The following part will illustrate the solutions related to these items.

a）Different Roles of the 3D Face. 
In Cite 2, constructing a 3D facial model is the ultimate goal. It begins with a generic 3D facial model and iteratively optimizes the shape of the corresponding 3D face by combining the differences in texture patterns around corresponding feature points from two 2D facial images taken from different angles.

Our invention uses features from a high-quality reference image to assist in the high-fidelity restoration of a low-quality input image. These image features include color, brightness, expression, pose, texture, and 3D geometry (not a 3D facial model). Specifically, the face color, brightness, expression, and pose remain consistent with the input image, while the facial 3D geometry and texture, including personalized features, are derived from the reference image. The 3D geometric features help establish spatial correspondence between the input image and the reference image, aiding in locating the corresponding reference features because the 3D geometric features remain consistent across different images of the same individual’s face.

b）Different Use of "Deformation". 
In Cite 2, [claim 3] mentions: "The method of claim 1, wherein the refining comprises: determining a pose deformation parameter and ***." Here, the deformation parameters refer to the continuous adjustment of the local shape of the 3D facial model. This is an optimization process aimed at making the final 3D facial model "more closely resemble" the input 2D facial image.

Our invention uses a deformation operator in the construction of negative samples. The purpose of this deformation operator is to introduce localized distortions in the 2D facial image, creating a face that does not exist in the real world (i.e., a "negative sample"). This guides the diffusion process to avoid generating artifacts (in simple terms, it tells the diffusion model that such distorted faces should not be generated).

c）Different Use of "Texture". 
In Cite 2, [claim 3] mentions: "*** reduce the difference in texture patterns between the first face image and the second face image in the peripheral area of the correspondence point.***" Here, the difference in texture patterns refers to mapping the constructed 3D facial model back to a 2D facial image and comparing it with the input 2D facial image. The texture difference is calculated in the surrounding areas of the same feature points on both 2D images. The feedback from this texture pattern difference is then used to continuously adjust and optimize the constructed 3D facial model. Simply put, the texture difference serves as a metric for optimizing the 3D facial model.

Our invention extracts texture features from a high-quality reference face to dynamically correct the intermediate latent features during the image enhancement process, ensuring that the final generated texture matches the texture of the reference face. In simple terms, the texture features from the reference face are the material and basis for improving texture fidelity.
3）Effects are different. 
The results of Cite 2 is 3D facial model while our invention result is a high-quality face image. As shown in Fig. 8, the Cite 2 converts a generic 3D facial model to a personalized 3D face model based on two or multi 2D input image at different views, while our invention improves the low-quality input images to the high-quality images with assistance of a high-quality reference image.

As a result, our invention cannot be derived from the combination of the one of Cite 1, Cite 2.
